# boa-firstrun.conf -- what this device should set itself up as.
#
# Copy to one of these, WITHOUT the .example, before the first boot:
#
#   /boot/boa-firstrun.conf      on x86 and the Pi, where the partition can be
#                                mounted from another machine and the file
#                                written before the device is ever switched on
#   /etc/boa-firstrun.conf       anywhere else: baked into the image, or copied
#                                over before the box is set up
#
# The box waits two minutes from boot for somebody to open the setup page. If
# nobody does, it reads this file and brings itself up from it.
#
# Every key is optional. Anything left out falls back to what the box would
# have chosen on its own -- a file naming only an SSID still gets a generated
# passphrase, so a half-filled file is not a half-configured box.
#
# THE FILE IS DELETED once it has been read: it carries a password in clear and
# its work is done. What it did is recorded in
# /etc/infinite-streaming-boa/firstrun instead, readable by root only.

# The network name. Left out, the box derives one from its board name and the
# last two octets of its LAN MAC -- cudy-3f16, qemu-1252. A MAC is in every
# beacon, so a name built from one gives nothing away.
#ssid=bench-3

# The passphrase, 8 to 63 characters. Left out, the box generates a random one
# per device and writes it into the firstrun record above, where you can read
# it over the wire. NEVER derived from the MAC, which anyone in radio range
# can read, and never a fixed default, which would be published credentials on
# every unit.
#key=choose something

# Two letters, or left out for the world domain. A country unlocks DFS
# channels, 2.4 GHz ch 12/13 and 3-6 dB of power. Without one the radios still
# work -- measured: both bands come up at 20 dBm. '00' is refused: hostapd
# rejects it as an invalid country_code and the access point never starts.
#country=GB

# The root password. THIS IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS. Without it the device comes
# up with no password at all -- anyone on the LAN can log in over SSH and into
# LuCI -- and `boa-setup check` goes on failing until a person sets one. A box
# cannot invent this for itself: a random one would lock you out, because there
# is no screen to read it from. A file can, because you chose it.
#root_password=

# Turn the device into a transparent bridge as the last step. Off by default:
# it deletes the wan interface, moves lan to DHCP and changes the device's
# address, so if the upstream network is not what you expect the box can end up
# somewhere you cannot reach. The old address is kept as a rescue interface
# either way. Run `boa-setup convert --dry-run` first if unsure.
#convert=no
